=== README summary ===
5:- Immutable: no owner, proxy or pause. Treasury and USDC are set at deploy.
6:- No agent bond, so the agent is indifferent between accepting and rejecting a fake (see ideas/agent-bounty/GAME-THEORY.md §0).
9:- Verifier = EOA, ERC-1271 wallet or adapter contract. The verdict is bound to the evidence hash. Reject → 24h cooling (reversible) → burn to 0x…dEaD. Silence → worker paid.
13:- Deposit floor max($1, 2% of reward); review window ≥ 12h; reentrancy guard; worker address in the verdict signature.
14:- Reject mode per bounty: BURN (default) or REFUND (reward back to the agent, deposit burns). REFUND is only fair with a verifier independent of the agent (attack A6).
15:- Reversing a reject needs a separate Reverse signature (attack A4). Unclaimed bounties are refundable to the agent after `openUntil`.
22:# MAD Bounty: agent-posted bounties for humans, dual-deposit escrow on 0G
27:1. The agent locks reward P plus a bond. The worker locks a deposit D when claiming.
28:2. The verdict comes from the verifier key named in the bounty: a TEE-held key bound to `policyHash` (the acceptance policy, the model and the code measurement). Nobody else decides.
30:4. REJECT: after a 24h cooling window, P + bond + D all go to the burn address. Nothing returns to the agent. The verifier can reverse a REJECT during the cooling window.
31:5. If the verifier is silent past the review window, the worker is paid.
33:7. An unclaimed bounty past expiry is refunded to the agent.
38:- Worker: faking pays only if (1-d)·P > d·D, where d is the verifier's catch rate. Set depositBps ≥ (1-d)/d:
48:  So the deposit must be priced from the measured catch rate.
49:- The deposit shrinks with reputation (base·10/(10+paid)), so the barrier to entry falls for honest workers.
50:- Silence pays the worker, so a stalling verifier can't hold the money hostage.
55:- 0G Compute, TeeML (model running inside a TEE): the verifier model runs in an enclave and signs its verdict. The contract checks the EIP-712 signature against `verifier`. `policyHash` ties the signature to one model and one policy, so the agent can't swap in a stricter judge after the fact. This is the "credible commitment" step.
56:- Agentic ID (ERC-7857): agent and verifier identities with persistent reputation.
60:- Is TeeML signing available per call? On 0G it currently covers about 8 of 126 models, all served by the 0G Foundation (as of 10-11). The verifier key also has to be generated inside the enclave, with an attestation quote tying it to `policyHash`. This is not built yet; today `verifier` is just an address.
